{-
   Threads, and the parking primitive everything else in this module is built on.

   Module `Native.Thread`. Under `Native` because every promise here is one the compiler cannot
   check, and named apart from it because a program that wants a mutex does not want the pointer
   arithmetic - `import Native.Thread` is the thing a caller writes, and it says what it is reaching
   for.

   § These are the raw, thread-specific primitives

   Everything here blocks an **operating-system thread**. That is the right primitive for a program
   whose concurrency is threads, and the wrong one for a program whose concurrency is fibers: a fiber
   that blocks its thread blocks every other fiber pinned to that thread's event loop. The
   fiber-aware forms will be the default ones and will not need this import; these will stay, under
   this name, for the code that means a thread.

   § What is not safe yet

   **The runtime is not thread-safe.** The heap is a global free list with no lock in front of it, so
   two threads allocating at once corrupt it - which means a thread body may not build a container,
   a string, or anything else that reaches the allocator. Integers, atomics, and storage the parent
   allocated before starting the thread are all fine. This is a fact about the runtime rather than
   about this module, and it is what a thread-safe allocator has to fix before any of this is usable
   for real work.

   There is also no thread-local storage: the new thread inherits the parent's, because nothing here
   sets up a new one.

   § Linux only, for now

   The whole file is `.linux.yana`, and the futex it is written over is `Native.Thread.Linux`. The
   split is where the port goes: everything here except `parkThread`, `unparkOne` and `unparkAll` is
   an algorithm any kernel that can block a thread supports.
-}
import Native
import Atomic
import Native.Thread.Linux

{-
   `parkThread(word, expected)` - stop this thread on `word`, unless it has stopped holding
   `expected`.

   The primitive, and the one operation in this module that could not be written in the language. It
   answers **nothing about why it returned**, and that is deliberate: a return means only "look
   again". A signal, a wake meant for another waiter, and a word that changed before the kernel read
   it are all indistinguishable to a correct caller, because a correct caller re-reads its own state.
   Every use of it in this module is inside a loop that does.

   What makes it a primitive rather than a convenience is the comparison, which happens **inside the
   kernel** under the queue's own lock. A thread that read the word, decided to wait, and then called
   a plain "sleep on this address" could be overtaken by a waker in between - the waker changes the
   word, looks for sleepers, finds none, and the waiter then sleeps on a condition that has already
   been satisfied. Passing the expectation in is what closes that window, and nothing in user space
   can close it for itself.
-}
pub fn parkThread(word: Atomic(U32), expected: U32) -> {}:
    futexWait(word, expected)

-- Start one waiter, if there is one. Answers whether anybody was actually started, which callers use
-- only for diagnostics: a release that wakes nobody is the ordinary uncontended case.
pub fn unparkOne(word: Atomic(U32)) -> Bool = futexWake(word, 1) > 0

-- Start every waiter, and answer how many. The right form when what changed makes progress possible
-- for all of them - a broadcast, a latch reaching zero, a write lock being released to readers.
pub fn unparkAll(word: Atomic(U32)) -> Size = futexWake(word, 0x7FFFFFFF)

{-
   A running thread, and the storage it is running on.

   One mapping holds both, which is what makes a thread one system call to start and one to give
   back. The first page is the control block - the word the kernel clears when the thread ends - and
   everything above it is the stack, growing down towards that page.

   **There is no guard page.** A thread that overflows its stack runs into its own control block and
   then off the bottom of the mapping, and only the second of those faults. Sizing the stack for the
   work is the caller's job, and 64 KiB is a great deal for anything this module can currently run.
-}
pub data Thread {region: %U8, size: Size, id: Size}

{-
   Where the kernel clears the thread's identifier, which is the first word of the control page.

   **As an atomic, and that is not decoration.** Another thread writes this word - the kernel does,
   from the exiting thread's own path - so every read of it is a read of memory this thread does not
   control. A plain load says the opposite: it is a load of ordinary memory, and an ordinary load in
   a loop whose body writes nothing the load depends on is one the optimizer may hoist out of the
   loop entirely. `join` was written that way, and what that compiles to at any optimization level
   willing to hoist it is a spin on a value read once, forever.

   `atomicAt` is exactly the operation for this: the word already exists, this is a *view* of it at
   the type that says how it may be read, and the borrow it hands back is rooted in the pointer so
   the ordinary rules apply to it. It is also what `parkThread` next door already takes, so the
   waiting and the reading now agree about what this word is.
-}
fn threadIdWord(return self: Thread) -> 'Atomic(U32) = atomicAt(self.region)

{-
   `createThread(entry, argument, stackBytes)` - a running thread, or `Nothing`.

   `entry` is an ordinary function of the language and a closure is admitted: both words of it cross
   to the new thread, which is what `cloneThread` is careful about. It is handed `argument` and
   nothing else, and when it returns the thread ends.

   **Nothing here owns `argument`.** It is a raw address, the thread reads it for as long as it
   likes, and keeping whatever it points at alive is the caller's problem - as is every other promise
   in this module.

   The stack is rounded up to a whole number of pages and the mapping carries the control page on
   top of it, so a caller asking for 64 KiB gets 64 KiB of stack.
-}
pub fn createThread(entry: (%U8) -> {}, argument: %U8, stackBytes: USize) -> Maybe(Thread):
    -- `alignUp` rather than the divide-multiply this used to write out, and `pageBytes` rather than
    -- the 4096 it repeated twice. The arithmetic is a mask on an unsigned word and a pair of shifts
    -- with sign fixups on a signed one, which is most of why `stackBytes` is `USize`: a stack size
    -- is a count of bytes and has no sign to carry.
    -- The control page sits under the stack - see above - so the mapping is the rounded stack
    -- plus one more page.
    -- The page as an unsigned word, which is the one place it is wanted in that sign - see the
    -- declaration in `Memory.native.yana`. A constant either way, so the conversion is not one.
    let page = bitcast(pageBytes) :: USize
    let size = alignUp(stackBytes, page) + page

    -- The same number as a signed word, once: the rounding above is unsigned because a byte count
    -- has no sign to carry, and everything below takes the width the language's pointer arithmetic
    -- and `mapMemory` are written in.
    let mapped = bitcast(size) :: Size

    let region = mapMemory(mapped)
    if isNull(region) then return Nothing

    -- One past the highest byte, which is where the child's stack starts and is page-aligned by
    -- construction - `cloneThread` needs sixteen and mmap gives four thousand and ninety-six.
    let top = region + mapped
    let id = cloneThread(cloneThreadFlags, top, entry, argument, bitcast(region) :: %U32)

    if id <= 0:
        unmapMemory(region, mapped)
        return Nothing

    return Just(Thread {region: region, size: mapped, id: id})

{-
   `join(thread)` - wait for it to end, and give its stack back.

   The wait is on the word `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` clears, and that is not an implementation detail
   but the whole reason joining is safe: the kernel clears it **after** the thread has stopped using
   its stack. A thread that published "I am done" itself would still be executing its own exit on the
   stack the joiner is about to unmap.

   The word is read before each wait rather than once, because the thread may have ended between the
   two - which is exactly the race `parkThread`'s expectation closes, asked here of a word the kernel
   writes instead of one this module does.

   Consumes the `Thread`, because what it answers is that there is no longer one.
-}
pub fn join(->self: Thread) -> {}:
    while True:
        let observed = threadIdWord(self).load(LoadAcquire)
        if observed == 0: break

        futexWaitTid(threadIdWord(self), observed)

    unmapMemory(self.region, self.size)

-- Whether the thread has ended, without waiting. Advisory in the way every unsynchronized
-- observation is: a `False` may be stale by the time it is read.
pub fn hasEnded(self: Thread) -> Bool = threadIdWord(self).load(LoadAcquire) == 0
